    Bug 2020627 - Implement application-layer watchdog to prevent POP3 queue deadlock on silent servers. r=mkmelin


    If a POP3 server drops a connection or goes completely silent without closing
    the TCP socket (e.g., after receiving AUTH LOGIN), the JavaScript POP3
    implementation enters a permanent deadlock. Because the client relies on an
    asynchronous DOM TCPSocket, the underlying nsSocketTransport's
    TIMEOUT_READ_WRITE NSPR timer does not tick while the socket is merely sitting
    idle waiting for an initial response.

    Since the client never times out, `_actionDone()` and `onFree()` are never
    called, causing the global `#_busyConnections` lock in Pop3IncomingServer to
    remain held forever, freezing all subsequent POP3 sync attempts across all
    accounts until the application is restarted.

    This patch fixes the deadlock by introducing an application-layer watchdog:
    - Starts a timer inside `_send()` whenever a command is sent to the network.
    - Clears the timer inside `_onData()` the moment the server proves it is alive.
    - If the timer fires, it triggers `_actionDone(Cr.NS_ERROR_NET_TIMEOUT)` to
      safely unwind the state machine, release the global queue lock, and free the
      connection.

    To prevent race conditions with the native C++ socket timeout during active,
    multi-line data streams (e.g., during RETR), the watchdog reads the user's
    `mailnews.tcptimeout` preference and adds a 10-second safety margin. This
    allows the OS to handle standard network timeouts natively, while acting as a
    fail-safe Time-To-First-Byte (TTFB) monitor for idle socket deadlocks.

    Differential Revision: https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D298240

